title: 15. Cleanup and resources (`defer`, move-only types, handles)
nav: Learn
order: 1500
template: docs
---
<p>Code that acquires something has to give it back: a file, a buffer, a lock, a half-written record. Bayan gives you two tools: <code>defer</code> runs a statement when a scope ends, and unique types, which own storage, are cleaned up by the compiler when their single owner goes away.</p>

<h2 id="defer">defer runs when the scope ends</h2>
<p>Write <code>defer</code> right after the thing it undoes. The statement runs when the scope exits, however it exits, and deferred statements run in reverse order.</p>
<example name="book-15-defer"/>
<p>In the output, <code>work("a")</code> prints <code>open a</code> and <code>using a</code>, then <code>close a</code> at the end of its body: the <code>defer</code> was written first but ran last. A <code>defer</code> in the loop belongs to the loop body, so <code>end of pass 0</code> prints before pass 1; the one in <code>main</code> runs after the last <code>print</code>. That is the nesting order of resources: the last opened is the first closed.</p>

<h2 id="failing">Cleanup that depends on how you leave</h2>
<p>Sometimes a step must only be undone on failure: a rollback, say. <code>defer failing</code> runs only when the function exits with an error, <code>defer panicking</code> only during a panic, and a plain <code>defer</code> in every case. Deferred statements cannot themselves fail.</p>
<example name="book-15-defer-failing"/>
<p>The first call prints <code>step ok</code>, then <code>always runs</code>. The second fails at <code>fail .broken</code>, so both defers run, <code>only when step fails</code> first because it was registered last, and the caller's <code>catch</code> prints <code>caught</code>. <code>defer failing</code> needs a function that can fail; <a href="errors.html">the errors guide</a> covers <code>fails</code> and <code>catch</code>.</p>

<h2 id="move-defer">defer and move</h2>
<p>A deferred statement counts as a use of every variable it mentions at scope exit. Move one away before then and the compiler stops you:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void consume(move List&lt;i32&gt; xs) {
}

void main() uses console, alloc {
    let xs = List&lt;i32&gt;()
    defer print($"count {xs.length}")
    consume(move xs)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2781:</span> `xs` is used after it was moved
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> defer-moved.byn:7:13

<span class="gut">  7 │</span>     consume(<span class="hl">move xs</span>)
<span class="gut">    │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^^ a `defer` on line 6 uses it at scope exit</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> move `xs` before the `defer`, or move a `.copy(to: alloc)` of it instead

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2781</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>defer-moved.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The error is on the <code>move</code>, and the label names the <code>defer</code> that still needs the value: move before the <code>defer</code>, or move a copy.</p>

<h2 id="unique">Unique types clean up for you</h2>
<p>A <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> or <code>StrBuf</code> owns a buffer; a struct holding one, or marked <code>struct unique</code>, is unique too. At scope exit, the value and everything it owns are released once by the final owner: no close call to write, none to forget. Chapter 13 showed how ownership moves between names.</p>
<p>So for a resource that owns memory: put it in a unique struct, pass it with <code>move</code> or by <code>ref</code>, let it go out of scope. At the end of <code>book-15-defer</code>, <code>t</code> moves into <code>moved</code> and its list is released when <code>main</code> ends. Use <code>defer</code> for what is not memory: a message, a rollback, a counter to put back.</p>
<p>A unique type cannot be copied by accident, so it cannot be released twice, which removes double-free and use-after-free from safe code. To drop a value early, move it to a function that takes <code>move</code> and returns.</p>
<note>Planned, not in the compiler yet: a cleanup hook you write for <code>struct unique</code>, such as closing a file descriptor at its owner's exit (a <code>close(move this)</code> is ordinary, and nothing calls it, so call it yourself or use <code>defer</code>); <code>Pool</code> and <code>Handle</code> types for stable references to pool items; typed indices such as <code>List&lt;T, RowId&gt;</code>. <code>Owned&lt;T&gt;</code> exists; <code>Index</code> is unknown.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">// planned
Pool&lt;Row&gt; rows = Pool&lt;Row&gt;()
Handle&lt;Row&gt; h = rows.add(Row { name: "a" })</code></pre>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. In <code>book-15-defer</code>, which line of output comes last, and why?</summary>
<p><code>main done</code>. A <code>defer</code> in <code>main</code> runs at exit, not where it is written; the loop's defers belong to the body, so each runs at the end of its pass.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. In <code>book-15-defer-failing</code>, why is <code>only when step fails</code> printed before the second <code>always runs</code>?
<ol type="a"><li>It is more important.</li><li>Defers run in reverse order and it was registered later.</li><li>The compiler sorts them alphabetically.</li></ol></summary>
<p>b. Last registered runs first; importance and alphabetical order play no part. On the success path <code>defer failing</code> never runs, so only <code>always runs</code> prints.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. You write <code>defer print($"{xs.length}")</code> and then <code>consume(move xs)</code>. What happens?</summary>
<p>Error BYN2781. The defer reads <code>xs</code> at scope exit, after the move, so it reports neither the length at the moment of the move nor an empty list. Move before the defer, or move <code>xs.copy(to: alloc)</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>4. Do you need a <code>close()</code> call for a struct holding a <code>List</code>?</summary>
<p>No. The struct is unique and its list is released with its owner; <code>defer</code> is for something outside memory, such as an external side effect.</p></details>

